
anchor Prompt_5c531f4d6a8a9918:
    userPrompt:
        Bring the spec located in spec/ up to date with everything I've prompted and
        you've built.
        
        1. Collect the history. .suspense/history holds one entry per prompt I've sent,
        named <unix-timestamp>-Prompt_<hash>. Older entries only have a .pi file,
        which contains the userPrompt. Newer entries have a .json file with a
        userPrompt field and an output array. The last type == "result" event in that
        array is the agent's final summary of what it did.
        Go through them in timestamp order.
        
        2. Fold them into the end state. Work oldest to newest and keep one running
        statement per part of the behaviour. In order to understand the categories of
        what's changed you need to read and understand everything, then break them
        into well-categorized chunks that relate to a known or unknown scope that can
        folder itself.
        A later prompt or result replaces whatever an earlier one said about the same
        part.
        My prompts say what I wanted; the result summaries say what was actually
        built.
        If a result skipped or misunderstood a request and a later prompt of mine
        corrected it, go with my correction. If nothing later addressed it, go with
        what I asked for. Leave out implementation workarounds, test details, and
        instructions about how the agent should work, as opposed to how the app should
        behave.
        
        3. Check against the current state. Read the spec under spec/ and the relevant
        code under src/. Take note what the spec already says correctly, so the
        prompt can skip it; what the spec is missing or has wrong, anything the code
        does or the prompts inform is true.
        
        4. Write the spec-agent prompt. Write it in my voice, as plain statements of how
        things should work now, with no history and no "change X to Y". Start by
        referencing the right scope, using an @\{ScopeName} reference. Group it under
        short bold headings. Include only what the spec is missing or has wrong, plus
        whatever context the agent needs to place it. If a system prompt or other
        text has to appear word for word, quote it exactly.
        
        5. After the prompt, list separately: behaviour that came up but belongs in a
        different scope, with the scope it belongs in; choices the agent made that I
        never asked for; anywhere my prompts contradict each other in a way that step
        2 couldn't settle.

    systemPrompt:
        We're working on the spec located in ./spec. Don't edit the code located in ./src.
